{:edb/db-before-t 2790 :edb/db-after-t 2791 :edb/tx-data [[13194139536103 50 #inst "2026-10-09T15:02:18.172Z" 13194139536103 true] [17592186129063 1020 #edb/ref 17592186383746 13194139536103 true] [17592186129063 1020 #edb/ref 17592186383753 13194139536103 true] [17592186129064 1020 #edb/ref 17592186383732 13194139536103 true] [17592186129064 1020 #edb/ref 17592186383739 13194139536103 true] [17592186129064 1020 #edb/ref 17592186383760 13194139536103 true] [17592186383732 1017 #edb/ref 17592186383733 13194139536103 true] [17592186383732 1089 "q-36951" 13194139536103 true] [17592186383732 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536103 true] [17592186383732 1100 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ of the matrix $A,$ given above. Find the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[a,\\,b,\\,c]^{T}$ such that the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\frac{b}{c}?$" 13194139536103 true] [17592186383732 1113 #uuid "6f6981a0-d3a3-5b77-bc40-10357c28936d" 13194139536103 true] [17592186383732 1145 "Since $A$ is a diagonal matrix, we have that its eigenvalues are the entries in the main diagonal, that is, ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$ \n\nAlso, since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ Thus, the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ must correspond to ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =-1.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the augmented matrix $M$ is already in row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd one). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the first and second equations, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$  Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{c}=\\frac{0}{1}=0.$" 13194139536103 true] [17592186383733 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383734 13194139536103 true] [17592186383733 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383735 13194139536103 true] [17592186383733 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383736 13194139536103 true] [17592186383733 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383737 13194139536103 true] [17592186383733 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383738 13194139536103 true] [17592186383733 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536103 true] [17592186383733 1041 "selection" 13194139536103 true] [17592186383733 1104 #edb/ref 17592186383737 13194139536103 true] [17592186383733 1110 #uuid "a86435e8-0336-5067-be16-7896bbcef262" 13194139536103 true] [17592186383734 1034 "2" 13194139536103 true] [17592186383734 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536103 true] [17592186383734 1111 #uuid "51e94431-303f-5369-81f2-7f340dda4c9f" 13194139536103 true] [17592186383735 1034 "1" 13194139536103 true] [17592186383735 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536103 true] [17592186383735 1111 #uuid "7948f3e5-1c3b-5f02-8a57-528d8dfe3aeb" 13194139536103 true] [17592186383736 1034 "4" 13194139536103 true] [17592186383736 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536103 true] [17592186383736 1111 #uuid "74efa85f-d981-5800-be8e-c5c09ee0fd8b" 13194139536103 true] [17592186383737 1034 "0" 13194139536103 true] [17592186383737 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536103 true] [17592186383737 1111 #uuid "5563e4e5-b373-51b6-9a91-78a5283ac83f" 13194139536103 true] [17592186383738 1034 "-1" 13194139536103 true] [17592186383738 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536103 true] [17592186383738 1111 #uuid "36cb282d-fc30-505f-9534-ef8613694720" 13194139536103 true] [17592186383739 1017 #edb/ref 17592186383740 13194139536103 true] [17592186383739 1089 "q-37401" 13194139536103 true] [17592186383739 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139536103 true] [17592186383739 1100 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2$ of the matrix $A,$ given above. Find the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[a,\\,b,\\,c]^{T}$ such that the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" 13194139536103 true] [17592186383739 1113 #uuid "45065b7f-0afe-5e15-9523-9e9af95aea68" 13194139536103 true] [17592186383739 1145 "First, we find all the eigenvalues of $A$ by solving the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )((-1-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )-(3)(2)) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -10) & =0 \\\\ -(\\lambda -1)(\\lambda +2)(\\lambda -5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=5.$ Since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ Thus, the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ must correspond to ${\\lambda }_{3}=5.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =5.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-6{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{5}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{12}{5}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{5}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139536103 true] [17592186383740 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383741 13194139536103 true] [17592186383740 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383742 13194139536103 true] [17592186383740 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383743 13194139536103 true] [17592186383740 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383744 13194139536103 true] [17592186383740 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383745 13194139536103 true] [17592186383740 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536103 true] [17592186383740 1041 "selection" 13194139536103 true] [17592186383740 1104 #edb/ref 17592186383741 13194139536103 true] [17592186383740 1110 #uuid "6b7da1ee-6beb-5fac-a2d3-8d69b31ae774" 13194139536103 true] [17592186383741 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139536103 true] [17592186383741 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536103 true] [17592186383741 1111 #uuid "d18d5a7a-4070-5222-a1fe-adeed6bc90b3" 13194139536103 true] [17592186383742 1034 "2" 13194139536103 true] [17592186383742 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536103 true] [17592186383742 1111 #uuid "bbce1985-5f7a-59eb-a470-972cefcad387" 13194139536103 true] [17592186383743 1034 "1" 13194139536103 true] [17592186383743 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536103 true] [17592186383743 1111 #uuid "54abc86b-1ded-5e17-918d-3f979fae4bec" 13194139536103 true] [17592186383744 1034 "4" 13194139536103 true] [17592186383744 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536103 true] [17592186383744 1111 #uuid "c5f6fe97-a629-50d7-a776-c965a4183125" 13194139536103 true] [17592186383745 103